WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.060
Your cortex is only about twice as thick as a mouse's.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.080
Yet its surface is roughly a thousand times larger.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.220
A leading explanation is more progenitors working for longer.

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.340
Outer radial glia renew themselves away from the ventricle.

5
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.160
Their daughters can become intermediate progenitors, which divide into neurons.

6
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.260
This is thought to amplify neuron production.

7
00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.680
More neurons help build a larger cortical sheet.

8
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:35.653
In living slices of human fetal cortex, researchers filmed these cells dividing.

9
00:00:36.613 --> 00:00:41.713
The classic outer radial glial cell keeps a fibre to the brain surface but has no

10
00:00:41.713 --> 00:00:43.073
contact with the ventricle.

11
00:00:43.993 --> 00:00:47.033
Daughters could divide again or become intermediate progenitors.

12
00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:51.473
These cells are abundant in primates and ferret, and rare in mouse.

13
00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:55.773
Their fibres fan the scaffold outward, favouring growth in area.

14
00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:58.593
The nearly smooth marmoset has them too.

15
00:00:58.593 --> 00:01:00.713
They do not guarantee folding.

16
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:04.663
Early founders provide another route to more area.

17
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:10.543
Pasko Rakic proposed the radial unit hypothesis from work in the fetal rhesus

18
00:01:10.543 --> 00:01:15.423
monkey. In this simplified model, each patch of progenitors makes a column.

19
00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:17.683
The number of units sets area.

20
00:01:18.523 --> 00:01:20.063
Their neuron output sets thickness.

21
00:01:21.303 --> 00:01:23.223
Related neurons can also spread sideways.

22
00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:28.067
While founders divide symmetrically, the pool doubles each round.

23
00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:32.807
In the simple model, three extra rounds give eight times as many units.

24
00:01:33.687 --> 00:01:37.987
Three extra direct asymmetric divisions add just three neurons per column.

25
00:01:38.927 --> 00:01:42.807
In mice, stabilized beta-catenin kept more progenitors cycling.

26
00:01:43.707 --> 00:01:48.387
The cortex enlarged and buckled, but this did not reproduce primate cortical growth.

27
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:52.567
Human-specific gene copies act on these processes.

28
00:01:53.567 --> 00:02:00.727
ARHGAP11B increased basal progenitors in mouse embryos and enlarged the neocortex in

29
00:02:00.727 --> 00:02:06.027
marmoset fetuses. NOTCH2NL kept progenitors dividing in mouse cortex.

30
00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:10.627
Deleting it made human organoid progenitors differentiate early.

31
00:02:11.487 --> 00:02:17.647
In cultured human fetal slices, blocking ARHGAP11B reduced basal progenitors.

32
00:02:18.447 --> 00:02:21.067
These tests support contributions to expansion.

33
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:24.427
None shows that one gene made the human cortex.

34
00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:31.770
Time matters too. Human cortical neurogenesis lasts longer than in rhesus monkeys or

35
00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.870
mice. That gives neurogenic progenitors more time to divide.

36
00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.030
Output also depends on cell-cycle length and division type.

37
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:42.650
Human neurons mature slowly too.

38
00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.590
Transplanted into newborn mouse cortex, they kept a largely human pace.

39
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:51.270
That is maturation, distinct from producing neurons.

40
00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.270
. . .

41
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.680
The result broadly fits primate scaling: a human brain holds about the neuron number

42
00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.800
predicted for a primate brain its size.

43
00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.600
Mass alone misses where those neurons sit.
